<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baptist War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Christmas Rebellion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baptist War, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Rebellion, was a rising of up to 60,000 enslaved people in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> that began on 25 December 1831. Led by the Baptist deacon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>, it started as a strike for wages and turned into an armed revolt in which many plantations were burned. It was crushed by early 1832 and hundreds were executed, but the reaction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Abolition Act of 1833.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baptist-war.jpg" alt="Baptist War" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adolphe Duperly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duperly_(1833)_Destruction_of_the_Roehampton_Estate_January_1832.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Many of the rebels had been educated by missionaries and had followed the progress of the abolitionist movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and their first aim was a peaceful general strike. Baptist slaves proved readier to act than Presbyterian, Wesleyan or Moravian converts, perhaps because white Baptist missionaries were often absent and Black deacons, left largely to themselves, reinterpreted Baptist teaching in the light of their own experience. When the missionary Thomas Burchell returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a> late in 1831, rumours spread that he carried documents signed by William IV granting full emancipation, and the governor's denial deepened the discontent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>, called Daddy by his followers, and his officers, literate slaves like himself, among them Robert Gardner, Thomas Dove, John Tharp and the deacon George Taylor, bound the workers by oath to stay away from work until they were paid half the going wage. Violence broke out on 27 December, when slaves burned the Kensington estate in the hills above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a>. Colonel William Grignon, a lawyer, landowner and militia commander, led the militia against them but was forced back by rebels armed with stockpiled guns, leaving Sharpe's forces in control of the rural Parish of St James.</p><p>Martial law was declared on 31 December. Sir Willoughby Cotton of the British Army enlisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> of Accompong Town, who after an early retreat at Catadupa defeated the rebels, killing Sharpe's deputy Campbell, and relieved Cotton's regulars when they were besieged at Maroon Town. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-maroons">Windward Maroons</a> of Charles Town and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moore-town">Moore Town</a> also pledged to fight, the rising collapsed, and the last rebels under Gardner surrendered. Fourteen white people were killed, and the rebels set fire to more than 100 properties, destroying over 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The reprisals were far more brutal than anything the rebels had done. An estimated 310 to 340 enslaved Jamaicans, Sharpe and Gardner among them, were put to death by various forms of judicial execution, some for offences as small as the theft of a pig. White mobs destroyed churches built for Black congregations, and the missionaries William Knibb and Henry Bleby were arrested and tarred and feathered before being released. Historians argue that this brutality hastened emancipation: the accounts Burchell and Knibb gave before the House of Commons outraged its members, and Parliament passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833">Slavery Abolition Act 1833</a>, followed by partial emancipation in 1834 and full emancipation in 1838.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baptist War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the British Caribbean the enslaved rose in Barbados in 1816 and Demerara in 1823, and at Christmas 1831 tens of thousands in western Jamaica followed the Baptist deacon Samuel Sharpe in a strike that became the <b>Baptist War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Baptist War</b>, also called the Christmas Rebellion, began on 25 December 1831 in western Jamaica.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance was constant, from Tacky's Revolt of 1760 to the <b>Baptist War</b> of 1831 to 1832 led by Samuel Sharpe, which hastened emancipation in 1834 to 1838.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-jamaica">Colony of Jamaica</a></li></ul></section>
